taking over a small hotel close to the  athletes village in tokyo  south korean chefs prepare meals for  their entire olympic team  it's aimed at keeping them more isolated  during the pandemic  but also to make sure no local  ingredients  carry radiation from the fukushima  nuclear plant disaster 10 years ago  we have put more effort in this time  because of the coronavirus concern and  because people are quite sensitive over  the origin of  ingredients such as those from fukushima  certainly the team members are all in  favor of the special menu  because they screen the ingredients i  prefer to eat more of this as i think  it's safer  but the food program has irritated many  in japan  which insists all its food is safe given  the long and troubled relationship  between japan and south korea  it doesn't take much to bring old  animosities to the surface  and the olympics has offered ample  opportunities  to do just that starting with the torch  relay  months ago the official map of japan  with the relay route  showed a tiny group of islets which are  disputed by the two neighbors  as being japanese known as dokdo in  south korea  a banner proudly proclaiming them to be  korean  hangs prominently opposite the japanese  embassy in seoul  before south korea's winter olympics in  2018  the same islets were initially shown as  being part of the korean peninsula  then a possible bridge-building visit to  tokyo by south korea's president moon  jae-in  was ruled out amid a controversy over  insulting remarks  made by a senior japanese diplomat and  back in the olympic village a banner  apparently evoking a 16th century korean  victory over japan  had to be removed following complaints  two old adversaries proving they are  happy to go head to head  whether olympic medals are involved or  not rob mcbride al jazeera  seoul
 

